ems/a93A03/lean/Main.lean"]},{"seq":6,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-27T06:42:07.046532110Z","text":"I see the issue clearly now. The `by_contra` approach with `liminf_eq` leads to circularity. But from the a96A03 work, I learned the right approach: use `liminf_le_of_le` with `IsBoundedUnder` constructed from nonnegativity (`⟨0, eventually_nonneg⟩`), which I successfully used there. Let me apply that lesson here.\n\nThe key insight from the a96A03 work: `IsBoundedUnder (· ≥ ·) atTop u = IsBounded (· ≥ ·) (map u atTop) = ∃ b, ∀ᶠ x in map u atTop, x ≥ b`. For `u n = ‖h n‖ ≥ 0`, the bound `b = 0` works trivially. Then `liminf_le_of_le h_bdd (fun b hb => ...)` proves `liminf ≤ a` when every eventual lower bound `b` satisfies `b ≤ a`.\n\nBut wait — I want to prove `‖x‖ ≤ liminf`, i.e., the OPPOSITE direction. I need `le_liminf_of_le` (lower bound on liminf), not `liminf_le_of_le` (upper bound on liminf). Let me check:\n\nThe helper `eventually_le_norm_of_weak` shows: for every `a < ‖x‖`, eventually `a ≤ ‖h n‖`. This means `a` is an eventual lower bound, so `a ≤ liminf`. Since this holds for all `a < ‖x‖`, we get `‖x‖ ≤ liminf` (by density of ℝ).\n\nThe right tool: for each `a < ‖x‖`, prove `a ≤ liminf` using `le_liminf_of_le`. This takes `IsCoboundedUnder` and `(∀ᶠ n, a ≤ u n)` and gives `a ≤ liminf`.\n\nFrom a96A03, I learned that `IsBoundedUnder (· ≥ ·)` (used by `liminf_le_of_le`) is trivially `⟨0, eventually_nonneg⟩`. But `le_liminf_of_le` needs `IsCoboundedUnder (· ≥ ·)` which is different. Let me check:"},{"seq":7,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-27T06:42:07.055843667Z","tools":["run_shell"],"previews":["run_shell cd /home/joe/code/apm-lean && grep -B1 -A5 \"theorem le_liminf_of_le\\b\" .lake/packages/mathlib/Mathlib/Order/LiminfLimsup"]},{"seq":8,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-27T06:42:56.477723974Z","text":"`le_liminf_of_le` takes `IsCoboundedUnder (· ≥ ·) f u` which is `(map u f).IsCobounded (· ≥ ·)`. And `IsCobounded r f = ∃ b, ∀ a, (∀ᶠ x in f, r x a) → r b a`.\n\nFor