use `le_of_forall_lt_iff_le` or work with the definition of liminf directly. The contradiction approach avoids needing `le_liminf_of_le`:\n\nIf `liminf < c` and `c` is an eventual lower bound, then `c ≤ sSup {a | eventually a ≤ u}`. But `liminf = sSup S`. So `c ≤ liminf`, contradicting `liminf < c`.\n\nFor `le_csSup` I need `BddAbove S`. From `liminf < c`: `sSup S = liminf < c`. If `S` weren't bounded above, `sSup S` would be `+∞` (not `< c`). So `S` IS bounded above. But Lean doesn't know this from the hypothesis.\n\nLet me try yet another approach — avoid `le_csSup` entirely. Use the property directly: if `c ∈ S` (i.e., `c` is an eventual lower bound), and `sSup S < c`, then there's an upper bound of `S` that's `< c`. But `c ∈ S` means `c` is in `S`, and any upper bound of `S` is `≥ c`. Contradiction!\n\nMore formally: `sSup S` is the LEAST upper bound of `S`. If `c ∈ S`, then `c ≤ sSup S` (since `sSup S` is an upper bound). So `liminf = sSup S ≥ c`, contradicting `liminf < c`.\n\nFor this, I need `sSup S` to be an upper bound of `S`, which requires `S` to be bounded above. But `sSup S = liminf < c < ∞` implies `S` is bounded above (otherwise `sSup S = +∞`). In a conditionally complete lattice, `csSup` requires `BddAbove`.\n\nThe cleanest fix: provide `BddAbove` from `‖h n‖ ≥ 0`. Wait, `BddAbove` means `∃ b, ∀ a ∈ S, a ≤ b`. Elements of `S` are `a` with eventually `a ≤ ‖h n‖`. Such `a` can be arbitrarily large (if `‖h n‖ → ∞`). So `S` might not be bounded above!\n\nUnless... …[trimmed]"},{"seq":11,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-27T06:44:06.780695044Z","tools":["edit_file"],"previews":["edit_file /home/joe/code/apm-lean/problems/a93A03/lean/Main.lean"]},{"seq":12,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-27T06:44:36.572063380Z","tools":["run_shell"],"previews":["run_shell cd /home/joe/code/apm-lean && lake env lean problems/a93A03/lean/Main.lean 2>&1 | head -20"]},{"seq":13,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-27T06:44:54.844362908Z","text":"Two issues:\n1. The `BddBelow` proof: `x✝